import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import ClaimClass from "@assayd/web-shared/ClaimClass.astro";

// The main commit this page and its plate were checked against.
const DEPICTS = "a1ce0bb";

const design03 =
  "https://github.com/Quinyte/assayd/blob/main/docs/designs/03-policy-compiler.md";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="The auth transaction — assayd"
  description="What the assayd operator does between an Agent being created and traffic reaching it: a resumable transaction that publishes a route only after an anonymous request has been refused."
  current="/concepts/the-auth-transaction.html"
>
  <article class="concept">
    <p class="kicker">Concept</p>
    <h1>The auth transaction</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      What the operator does between “I created an Agent” and “traffic
      reaches it”.
    </p>

    <p class="thesis">
      An Agent’s authentication is applied as a resumable transaction that
      publishes its route only after an anonymous request through it has
      actually been refused.
      <ClaimClass claim="measured" test="TestCreatePublishesOnlyOnA401" />
    </p>

    <p>
      The reason fits in a sentence. A route that carries traffic before the
      gateway has taken its policy is a route serving unauthenticated, and a
      write that succeeded, or a status that reads “accepted”, is not the same
      thing as a refusal observed through the route.
    </p>

    <p>
      Most of the evidence on this page is an envtest: a real Kubernetes API
      server with no kubelet and no gateway, where the anonymous request is
      answered by a stub and the Gateway’s status is written by the test. The
      end-to-end tests, on k3d against agentgateway 1.5.0, are named where
      they exist.
    </p>

    <h2 id="stages">The stages</h2>
    <p>
      The transaction is recorded at <code>status.auth.transaction</code>. Its
      stage values are the seven that <code>internal/controller/authtxn.go</code>
      defines, read for this page at <code>main</code>
      <code>{DEPICTS}</code>. Nothing fails if one is added, renamed or removed:
      the CRD field carries no enum, and no test pins the list.
      <ClaimClass claim="not-built" note="no test or schema pins the stage vocabulary" />
    </p>

    <dl class="stages">
      <dt><code>PreparingRoute</code></dt>
      <dd>
        A new API-key Agent’s route is written first with no
        <code>backendRefs</code>, so it exists and carries nothing.
        <ClaimClass claim="measured" test="TestCreatePublishesOnlyOnA401" />
      </dd>

      <dt><code>ProbingBefore</code></dt>
      <dd>
        Only a <code>Lock</code> starts here, on a route that is already
        serving. It sends one anonymous request and records the answer before
        writing anything. It is an observation, not a gate.
        <ClaimClass claim="measured" test="TestJ2LocksAServedNoneAgentInPlace" />
      </dd>

      <dt><code>ApplyingPolicies</code></dt>
      <dd>
        The <code>&lt;agent&gt;-auth</code> policy is written. The status update
        that enters this stage marks the transaction written before the write
        happens, so a crash between the two re-enters here and repeats the
        write, which is idempotent.
        <ClaimClass claim="measured" test="TestCreateReentersAtTheWriteAfterACrash" />
      </dd>

      <dt><code>Converging</code></dt>
      <dd>
        The transaction waits until the Gateway reports the route and the
        policy converged, and the route stays unpublished while it waits.
        <ClaimClass claim="measured" test="TestACreateHasADeadlineOutcomeInEveryStage" />
      </dd>

      <dt><code>ProbingAfter</code></dt>
      <dd>
        An anonymous request goes through the route, and the transaction stays
        here until the answer is a <code>401</code> it is allowed to credit.
        Converged conditions alone never publish the route.
        <ClaimClass claim="measured" test="TestCreatePublishesOnlyOnA401" />
      </dd>

      <dt><code>Publishing</code></dt>
      <dd>
        The <code>backendRefs</code> go onto the route. An Agent that declares
        <code>auth: none</code> starts here: its route is published at once,
        labelled <code>assayd.dev/auth: "none"</code>, with no policy written
        and no request sent.
        <ClaimClass claim="measured" test="TestANoneAgentIsPublishedAtOnceWithItsMarker" />
      </dd>

      <dt><code>Refused</code></dt>
      <dd>
        Only an <code>Adopt</code> sits here. A route published before the
        compiler existed is refused rather than adopted. It stays published and
        unauthenticated, under <code>GovernanceSkipped</code> with reason
        <code>CompilerUpgradeUnsupported</code>, and no policy is written onto
        it.
        <ClaimClass claim="measured" test="TestARoutePublishedBeforeTheCompilerIsNotLocked" />
      </dd>
    </dl>

    <h2 id="served">Served is not a stage</h2>
    <p>
      There is no <code>Served</code> stage value. An Agent is served when
      <code>status.auth.mode</code> is recorded and the transaction slot is
      empty, so the operator ends a transaction by clearing it rather than by
      advancing it.
      <ClaimClass claim="measured" test="TestCreatePublishesOnlyOnA401" />
    </p>

    <h2 id="entry-points">Not every Agent walks the whole chain</h2>
    <p>
      Where a transaction starts depends on what it is for. A new Agent’s
      <code>Create</code> is the chain above; these are the others.
    </p>
    <ul class="claims">
      <li>
        A served API-key Agent whose route is deleted gets it back through a
        new <code>Create</code> taken from its recorded status. The route is
        prepared with no <code>backendRefs</code> and is published again only
        after a new <code>401</code>.
        <ClaimClass claim="measured" test="TestADeletedRouteIsPreparedAgainAndPublishedOnlyOnA401" />
      </li>
      <li>
        A served <code>auth: none</code> Agent edited to <code>apikey</code> is
        locked in place. Its route keeps serving throughout, and the new mode is
        recorded only after the answer through that route turns to
        <code>401</code>.
        <ClaimClass claim="measured" test="TestJ2LocksAServedNoneAgentInPlace" />
      </li>
      <li>
        On k3d against agentgateway 1.5.0, that lock runs end to end: the same
        route stops answering anonymous callers and keeps answering with a key.
        It is skipped where no gateway is installed.
        <ClaimClass claim="measured" test="TestAServedNoneAgentIsLockedInPlace" />
      </li>
      <li>
        One way a refused <code>Adopt</code> ends is the owner’s edit: the owner
        sets <code>auth</code> to <code>none</code>, waits for the refusal to
        record it, and then sets <code>apikey</code>. That runs the same kind
        of lock.
        <ClaimClass claim="measured" test="TestK2AnOwnersEditEndsTheRefusal" />
      </li>
      <li>
        A served API-key Agent whose policy is deleted gets the policy back by a
        <code>Lock</code>. The policy is rebuilt from the recorded status, the
        route is left open, and the policy is trusted again only after a
        <code>401</code> that can be attributed to it.
        <ClaimClass claim="measured" test="TestADeletedPolicyIsRecreatedAndTrustedOnlyOnAnAttributed401" />
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="waits">What makes it wait</h2>
    <ul class="claims">
      <li>
        While a policy on the Gateway itself could have answered the anonymous
        request, no <code>401</code> is credited. The transaction holds at
        <code>ProbingAfter</code> with reason <code>GatewayAuthPolicy</code>,
        and moves on once that policy goes.
        <ClaimClass claim="measured" test="TestACreateHoldsWhileAGatewayLevelAuthPolicyStands" />
      </li>
      <li>
        On k3d against agentgateway 1.5.0, a new Agent beside a Gateway-level
        API-key policy holds at <code>ProbingAfter</code> on the Gateway’s own
        <code>401</code>. It is skipped where no gateway is installed.
        <ClaimClass claim="measured" test="TestAGatewayLevelAuthPolicyHoldsANewAgent" />
      </li>
      <li>
        A NACK Event from agentgateway’s controller on the Gateway returns a
        <code>Lock</code> to <code>Converging</code>, and it does not finish
        while that NACK stands.
        <ClaimClass claim="measured" test="TestANackReturnsALockToConverging" />
      </li>
      <li>
        A transaction’s deadline is a condition, not a timeout. Past it, a
        <code>Create</code> reads <code>PolicyApplyIncomplete=True</code> with a
        message naming the stage it waits in, and <code>Ready=False</code>, and
        stays where it is, unpublished.
        <ClaimClass claim="measured" test="TestACreateHasADeadlineOutcomeInEveryStage" />
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="one-way">The lock is one-way</h2>
    <p>
      Editing a served API-key Agent back to <code>auth: none</code> is refused.
      The served policy is left untouched, the Agent reads
      <code>PolicyCompileFailed=True</code> with reason
      <code>AuthTransitionNotBuilt</code>, and <code>status.auth.mode</code>
      still reads <code>apikey</code>.
      <ClaimClass claim="measured" test="TestAPIKeyToNoneIsRefused" />
    </p>

    <h2 id="limits">What this does not guarantee</h2>
    <p>
      Each limit below is named and linked to the section of design 03 that
      describes it. None is given with steps to reproduce it.
    </p>
    <ul class="claims">
      <li>
        One request proves one gateway replica, and no replica count is
        declared. A served API-key Agent says so:
        <code>GovernanceSkipped=False</code>, reason
        <code>AuthVerifiedOnOneReplica</code>.
        <ClaimClass claim="measured" test="TestAPassOnOneReplicaIsReadyAndAHeldSwitchIsNot" />
      </li>
      <li>
        Nothing shows that the gateway’s proxy has applied a policy. The
        operator reads the Gateway’s control-plane status, which the proxy may
        lag, so its checks narrow that window and do not close it
        (<a href={`${design03}#332-what-the-barrier-can-and-cannot-prove`}>design 03 §3.3.2</a>).
        <ClaimClass claim="not-built" note="no check reads the proxy's own configuration" />
      </li>
      <li>
        A policy deleted in the short interval between a pass’s check and its
        route write leaves the route published without it until the next pass
        (<a href={`${design03}#333-in-place-tightening-is-trusted-only-on-a-probe`}>design 03 §3.3.3</a>).
        <ClaimClass claim="not-built" note="design 03 §3.3.3 records the window; nothing closes it" />
      </li>
      <li>
        Design 03 §3.5 specifies token and USD budgets compiled onto the
        gateway. Nothing compiles one, and a stored <code>spec.budget</code> is
        refused. No rate limit or tool allowlist is compiled either.
        <ClaimClass claim="designed" design="design 03" section="§3.5" />
      </li>
      <li>
        Nothing pages. Every condition on this page is something a person has
        to go and read.
        <ClaimClass claim="not-built" note="design 10 is not built, and no alert rule ships" />
      </li>
    </ul>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

